BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Stanford Law] Building a Practice of Positive Change: The Injunct
 ive and Informational Effect of Tort Law
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270423
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270425
DTSTAMP:20261119T211238Z
UID:stanford_campus-law-10003432-1808438400-1808611199@law.stanford.edu-20
 27-04-23T00:00:00-07:00@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Tort law is often seen as a matter of money. But tort lawsuits
  can accomplish much more. They can bring misconduct and hidden dangers to
  light\, force defendants to change their policies\, practices\, or produc
 ts\, and catalyze government reform\, all of which protect the public long
  after a case ends. In their papers\, How Tort Law Drives Change and The I
 njunctive Effect of Tort Law\, Professor Nora Freeman Engstrom of Stanford
  Law School and Lisa Qian ’26 coin these effects the informational and i
 nju\n\nAges: All ages\nSource: https://law.stanford.edu/event/building-a-p
 ractice-of-positive-change-the-injunctive-and-informational-effect-of-tort
 -law/\nLast synced: 2026-11-19 13:12 PST
LOCATION:@ SLS: Room 290\, 559 Nathan Abbott Way\, Stanford\, CA\, 94305-8
 610\, United States
URL:https://law.stanford.edu/event/building-a-practice-of-positive-change-
 the-injunctive-and-informational-effect-of-tort-law/
END:VEVENT
